Deeper in the wilderness, Zhily and Jily discovered a mysterious sequence of numbers. Each prefix of the sequence has an important characteristic value, known as the mex and the max. By rearranging the sequence, it can generate a special kind of magic.
You are given an array a of n non-negative integers. You can rearrange it arbitrarily.
Find the maximum possible value of the sum of the MEX and the maximum value over all prefixes. Formally, you need to maximize the sum from i=1 to n of (mex(a1, a2, ..., ai) + max(a1, a2, ..., ai)).
The minimum excluded (MEX) of a collection of integers c1, c2, ..., ck is defined as the smallest non-negative integer x which does not occur in the collection c.

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t. The description of the test cases follows.
The first line of each test case contains a single integer n, the length of the array a.
The second line contains n integers a1, a2, ..., an, the array a.

Output:
For each test case, output the maximum sum on a new line.

Constraints:
1 <= t <= 10000
1 <= n <= 200000
0 <= ai <= 1000000000
The sum of n over all test cases does not exceed 200000.

Strategy:
Here the biggest leap in logic that needs to be made is that the best possible strategy is to put the biggest number in the array to index 0 (which maximizes all the prefix sums totaling m*n) and then maximize the running mex by using the numbers in order and not putting duplicates next to each other. This gives us 2 possible cases. One where the maximum number is also a part of the 1 to x series and therefore the mex for the array is max+1. The second case is where there is a gap between the max and other smaller numbers and therefore the mex increasing part will end earlier. This gives us 2 equations for which we need to obtain variables which are the maximum mex, the maximum number and the number of duplicates in the array. Then we check if max < mex. If it is it means it must be a part of the increasing sequence and therefore the total output will me max*n for maxes and then for mex it is (here mex is the maximum mex) (mex-1)*(mex-2)/2+mex*(duplicates+1). The first part is the sum of the mexes all the way to mex-2. This is because we have used the max at the start and therefore it is included in all the prefixes which means the mex will increase by 1 by adding a number all the way until we add max-2 included which corresponds to mex-2. We cannot count mex-1 since when we add max-1 the mex immediately jumps to max+1 because of the max at the start. Therefore then we will have mex*(duplicates+1) here we can get duplicates by taking n-mex. This is the result for max < mex. For max >= mex we have that that max is not included in the sequence of increasing number where we increase mex. The equation here is max*n + (mex+1)*(mex)/2 since we go all the way from 0 to mex one by one. And then the rest is mex*(n-mex-1) Here n-mex is the duplicates and numbers unused in the mex and we need to remove 1 because we need to account for the max used at the start. We output one of these based on the max <-> mex relation.